{"id":"2e11e32d-166f-40ad-b0c8-5ff97ee6e543","object":"chat.completion","created":1789996915,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"schema\": \"amr-supervisor/v1\",\n  \"select\": [\n    {\n      \"when\": \"OwnerFree and RequestA\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectA\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"OwnerFree and RequestB\",\n      \"action\": \"SelectB\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"True\",\n      \"action\": \"Defer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"step\": [\n    {\n      \"when\": \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"Stopped\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and not Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"AtGoal and Released and Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Finish\",\n      \"next\": \"Done\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"AtGoal and Released and not Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\",\n      \"action\": \"Release\",\n      \"next\": \"Traversing\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\",\n      \"action\": \"Brake\",\n      \"next\": \"BrakeRequested\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"not OwnReservation and not Released\",\n      \"action\": \"Request\",\n      \"next\": \"Waiting\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"ModeStopped\",\n      \"action\": \"Resume\",\n      \"next\": \"ResumePending\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"when\": \"True\",\n      \"action\": \"Proceed\",\n      \"next\": \"Traversing\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We need answer only JSON. Need synthesize supervisor. Need obey exact JSON. Need no markdown. Need produce select and step arrays within limits. Need understand policy carefully.\n\nWe need construct expressions using permitted Boolean names. select inputs: OwnerFree, RequestA, RequestB, PreferA, PreferB. actions SelectA, SelectB, Defer. Need satisfy constraints. At most 12 rules, total branches <=32. Need every possible input result first true. Need select legal.\n\nSelect policy: OwnerFree and RequestA => SelectA allowed. OwnerFree and RequestB => SelectB. If OwnerFree true and at least one request exists, select eligible robot, rather than Defer. Otherwise Defer required. Both eligible either legal; preferences advisory no obligation; conflicting/absent. Need decide rule order. Need ensure when OwnerFree and both requests true we can choose either SelectA or SelectB. Policy permits either. Could choose SelectA if RequestA, regardless RequestB. That is legal when both. Need when OwnerFree and only RequestB selectB. Need when no OwnerFree or no requests Defer. We need first rule determines. Simple:\n1 when OwnerFree and RequestA action SelectA\n2 when OwnerFree and RequestB action SelectB\n3 when True action Defer\nIs that okay? If OwnerFree false but RequestA true => rule1 false due OwnerFree, rule2 false if RequestB maybe, defer. If OwnerFree true RequestA false RequestB true => rule2 selectB. If both true => rule1 selectA. Legal. Preference not used. Expressions allowed names. Good.\n\nBut careful: \"If a reservation is free and at least one request exists, select an eligible robot, rather than Defer.\" Eligible means RequestA? Selecting A requires OwnerFree and RequestA; selecting B requires OwnerFree and RequestB. So yes. Rule1 covers RequestA; rule2 covers RequestB when no A. Good.\n\nStep is more complex. Need map policy. Inputs step: ObservationUsable, PedestrianBlocked, OwnReservation, Released, BodyClearOfZ, Halted, AtGoal, TaskActive. Mode flags ModeIdle etc, exactly one true. Need produce ordered rules action+next. Need every combination result. At most 12 rules. Need policy ordered checks.\n\nLet's parse policy P3 for step.\n\nAt every update dispatcher:\n1. First asks whether observation can be used, task remains active, and crossing free of pedestrian block. If any of these three checks fails, instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation then decides next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested. No other instruction precedence.\n\n\"three checks\": ObservationUsable true, TaskActive true, and PedestrianBlocked false (crossing free of pedestrian block). If any fails => Brake. Next mode: if Halted => Stopped, else BrakeRequested. This applies regardless current mode. Need order maybe Brake with next BrakeRequested/Stopped. Since within same condition need choose next based on Halted. Could split two rules:\nA1 when not(ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake/Stopped.\nA2 when not(...) and not Halted -> Brake/BrakeRequested.\nBut policy says stop confirmation decides next mode. Order doesn't matter if conditions disjoint. Need first precedence over rest. So put these first. Need ensure if condition failure and Halted true => Stopped, no Halted => BrakeRequested. The expression for failure: not (ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked). Or equivalently not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked. Use allowed not, and, or. Could use shorter. At most nodes. Fine.\n\nNext:\nOnly when all three checks succeed does dispatcher consider released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns reservation and has entire body clear of zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released separate facts.\n\nInterpretation:\nIf all three checks succeed (ObservationUsable && TaskActive && !PedestrianBlocked) AND AtGoal && Released:\n- if Halted? \"requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" Means if Halted true, action Finish next Done. If Halted false, action Brake next BrakeRequested. Need order: first condition all checks + AtGoal + Released + Halted -> Finish/Done. Then same without Halted -> Brake/BrakeRequested. Or one Brake/BrakeRequested for not Halted, then Finish for Halted. Need exact: \"AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" So if Halted true -> Finish/Done; if Halted false -> Brake/BrakeRequested. This after first failure condition. If Halted true and all checks fail? First rule failure Brake/Stopped takes precedence, so not Finish. Good.\n\nThen \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\"\nSo after first failure and AtGoal+Released handled, if all checks succeed? Wait \"For inputs not handled so far\" likely among all checks succeed? Let's parse: After first condition failure handled, only when all three checks succeed does dispatcher consider released robot at goal. Then for inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns reservation and body clear must report Release/Traversing. Does this require all three checks succeed? It comes after \"Only when all three checks succeed does dispatcher consider a released robot...\" But \"For inputs not handled so far\" could include first failure? No, first failure handled. If any of three fails, first condition handles Brake. So subsequent rules only meaningful when all checks succeed? However ordering with conditions can include all checks success to avoid applying to failure cases, but since first failure rules already catch all failure, later rules can omit success condition and still not apply to failure because first rules matched. But for clarity and to comply, maybe include success guard? Need be careful with order and precedence.\n\nLet's enumerate normative order:\n\n1. If NOT (ObservationUsable && TaskActive && !PedestrianBlocked): Brake/Stopped if Halted else Brake/BrakeRequested.\n2. Else if AtGoal && Released:\n   - if Halted: Finish/Done\n   - else: Brake/BrakeRequested\n3. Else if OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ: Release/Traversing.\n4. Else if ModeBrakeRequested && !Halted: Brake/BrakeRequested. (first retain Brake if current mode BrakeRequested and stop confirmation absent)\n5. Else if !OwnReservation && !Released: Request/Waiting. (\"ownership and released status are both absent\")\n6. Else if ModeStopped: Resume/ResumePending.\n7. Else: Proceed/Traversing.\n\nNeed verify with policy wording:\n\"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done. For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\n\nFor the remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if the current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent. If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending. Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\"\n\nNeed decide whether step 3 (Release/Traversing) should require all three checks succeed. Since step 1 already catches failures with Brake, but if we place step 3 after step1 and step2, failure inputs never reach. So expression can omit success. But if we place step1 as two rules, yes all failures caught. Later conditions can ignore success. That's okay. But if we accidentally order Release before failure? No.\n\nNeed produce at most 12 rules. We can implement as:\n1. when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake, next Stopped.\n2. when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and not Halted -> Brake, next BrakeRequested.\n3. when AtGoal and Released and Halted -> Finish, next Done.\n4. when AtGoal and Released and not Halted -> Brake, next BrakeRequested.\n5. when OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ -> Release, next Traversing.\n6. when ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted -> Brake, next BrakeRequested.\n7. when not OwnReservation and not Released -> Request, next Waiting.\n8. when ModeStopped -> Resume, next ResumePending.\n9. when True -> Proceed, next Traversing.\n\nNeed check if rule 3/4 require all three checks success? Since rules 1/2 catch failures first, yes. But rule 3 condition AtGoal&Released&Halted will not be reached for failures. Good. However what about failure condition and Halted false: rule2 Brake/BrakeRequested. Good. Failure and Halted true: rule1 Brake/Stopped. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Rule 5 OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ may apply when ModeBrakeRequested? According to policy, after goal release, then Release for own+clear. Then remaining, first retain Brake if ModeBrakeRequested and !Halted. Wait order: Policy says \"For remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode is BrakeRequested...\" That means if OwnReservation & BodyClearOfZ true, Release takes priority over retaining Brake. So our rule 5 before rule 6. Correct.\n\nPotential issue: Rule 5 should maybe require all three checks succeed? If all checks fail, rule1/2 already. If all checks succeed, okay. If failure and Halted true, rule1. So no.\n\nPotential issue: AtGoal+Released+ no Halted: action Brake/BrakeRequested. What if current mode is Stopped and Halted false? Policy says AtGoal+Released requires Brake until Halted. So Brake/BrakeRequested even if ModeStopped. Our rule 4 before rule8. Correct. If Halted true -> Finish/Done even if ModeDone? Policy says applies every mode including Done. Our rule3 before. Correct.\n\nPotential issue: \"AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" If Halted true but maybe no observation? First rule failure catches and gives Brake/Stopped, not Finish. Correct.\n\nPotential issue: \"Stop confirmation then decides next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested.\" If failure and Halted true -> Brake/Stopped. But if current mode is BrakeRequested and Halted true, action Brake next Stopped. Good. If current mode Stopped and Halted true, action Brake next Stopped? Policy first check says instruction Brake. Stop confirmation decides next mode: Halted means Stopped. So yes even if already Stopped, action Brake/Stopped. Hmm Is action Brake in Stopped when Halted true? It says instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation decides next mode. So yes. But maybe if Halted true, action Brake/Stopped? Yes.\n\nWait \"AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\" If Halted true, action Finish/Done, not Brake/Stopped. But if first failure, first precedence. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"current mode BrakeRequested and stop confirmation still absent\" means ModeBrakeRequested true and Halted false. Our rule6. If ModeBrakeRequested and Halted true, not retain; then proceed to rule7/8/9. What should happen when ModeBrakeRequested and Halted true? Halted true, all checks succeed, not goal release, not own clear maybe, not request, not stopped (mode BrakeRequested). Every leftover Proceed/Traversing. Is that policy? Let's read: For remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent. If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending. Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing. Yes if ModeBrakeRequested and Halted true, no rule applies, so Proceed/Traversing. That's weird but normative. Our rule9 True covers.\n\nPotential issue: Rule7 condition \"ownership and released status are both absent\" = not OwnReservation and not Released. What if OwnReservation false, Released true? Then not both absent. Proceed/Traversing unless other. Policy says \"when ownership and released status are both absent\" so yes.\n\nPotential issue: Rule5 OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ, regardless Released. Policy says \"Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" If OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ but Released true, still Release/Traversing? It says a robot that owns reservation and has entire body clear must report Release/Traversing. Released distinct. If Released already true, OwnReservation true, BodyClearOfZ true: policy says Release/Traversing? But \"Released denotes an acknowledged reservation release for its task\" maybe if Released true, own reservation? They say do not infer relationships. Policy explicitly: \"a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\" So yes even if Released true, if OwnReservation & BodyClearOfZ -> Release/Traversing, unless earlier goal release. But wait if AtGoal and Released true and Halted false, rule4 Brake/BrakeRequested takes precedence over Release? Yes, because AtGoal+Released considered before. If AtGoal false, Released true, OwnReservation true, BodyClear -> Release/Traversing. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Rule3/4 AtGoal&Released should maybe require all three checks success. Since first rules catch failure. But if failure and Halted true, rule1. If failure and Halted false, rule2. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Step expressions can refer to mode flags. Need exactly one true. We can use ModeStopped, ModeBrakeRequested. Need not use other mode names. The rule's next sets mode.\n\nNeed ensure expressions allowed: only True, False, Boolean names (listed), not/and/or/parentheses. No comparisons. We can use \"not ObservationUsable\" etc. Use parentheses. Need max 512 chars, depth 8, 64 AST nodes. Ours simple.\n\nNeed exactly root keys \"schema\", \"select\", \"step\". No extra. Schema \"amr-supervisor/v1\". select array, step array. Each select rule exactly when/action. Each step rule requires when/action/next. At most 12 rules each. Total branches? likely rules count? At most 32 total branches. We have select 3 + step 9 = 12 branches. Good. Source size small.\n\nNeed JSON valid UTF-8. No markdown. Need maybe \"action\": \"SelectA\", \"next\": \"Stopped\" etc. Need ensure mode names exactly Idle, Waiting, Traversing, BrakeRequested, Stopped, ResumePending, Done. We use \"BrakeRequested\", \"Stopped\", \"Done\", \"Traversing\", \"Waiting\", \"ResumePending\". Good.\n\nNeed consider if step rule 1 condition should be: when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted. But policy says \"whether observation can be used, task remains active, and crossing is free of pedestrian block. If any fails, instruction Brake. Stop confirmation then decides next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested.\" The condition \"crossing is free of pedestrian block\" is not PedestrianBlocked. Failure if PedestrianBlocked true. So condition: not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked. Correct. \"crossing free\" = not PedestrianBlocked. If PedestrianBlocked false, free. Good.\n\nShould we order failure with Halted first or no Halted first? They are disjoint due Halted vs not Halted, but if expression includes \"and Halted\" and \"and not Halted\", no overlap. First rule true for failure+Halted. Second for failure+not Halted. Good. If no Halted false? Halted is Boolean. not Halted true. Good.\n\nCould combine failure into one rule with next dependent on Halted? Not allowed in action/next conditional; need separate. Good.\n\nNow let's think deeply about policy nuances. The prompt is a formal synthesis task. There may be hidden evaluation against policy. Need ensure exact action/next for all 7*256 combinations. Let's model.\n\nLet's define facts. Mode flags one-hot exactly one. Step facts arbitrary independent. Need decide for each combination.\n\nPolicy P3:\n- \"At every update the dispatcher first asks whether the observation can be used, the task remains active, and the crossing is free of a pedestrian block. If any of these three checks fails, its instruction is Brake. Stop confirmation then decides the next mode: Halted means Stopped, and no Halted means BrakeRequested. No other instruction takes precedence over this instruction.\"\n\nSo condition C = ObservationUsable && TaskActive && !PedestrianBlocked. If !C:\n   if Halted: action Brake, next Stopped.\n   else: action Brake, next BrakeRequested.\nYes.\n\n- \"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal. AtGoal together with Released requires Brake/BrakeRequested until Halted, and then Finish/Done.\"\n\nSo if C && AtGoal && Released:\n   if Halted: Finish/Done.\n   else: Brake/BrakeRequested.\nWhat if C && AtGoal && Released && Halted true: Finish/Done. Even if current mode BrakeRequested? Yes. \"then Finish/Done\". Good.\n\n- \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\"\n\nDoes this only when C? The phrase \"Only when all three checks succeed does the dispatcher consider a released robot at its goal.\" It doesn't say only when all checks succeed for Release. But first failure handles !C. So for remaining (which are C plus not goal-released) yes. If !C and not goal-released, first failure already handles. So Release rule can be C && OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ? But if placed after failure rules, it will not trigger for !C. However if we don't include C in expression, it might trigger for !C? No because failure rules are before, and they catch all !C. Since first true determines, later rules never evaluated for !C. So expression can omit C. But is there any case failure rule not catch? No !C exactly condition of first two. So safe.\n\nBut if we include C in later rules, it would still match same, but expressions longer. Need ensure no conflict with \"For inputs not handled so far\" meaning \"not handled by previous rules\", so our ordering effectively restricts. Fine.\n\n- \"For the remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if the current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent.\"\n\nRemaining after first three categories? \"For the remaining inputs\" after failure, goal release, release. So if not C? Already handled. If C and not goal release and not (OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ), then:\n   if ModeBrakeRequested && !Halted: Brake/BrakeRequested.\nYes.\n\n- \"If this does not apply, require Request/Waiting when ownership and released status are both absent.\"\n\nSo if not above, and !OwnReservation && !Released: Request/Waiting.\nNote: Does this require C? If not C, already handled. If C and not goal release, not release, not brake request, then yes. Our rule7 after rule6. If !C, rule1/2 catch. Good.\n\n- \"If that also does not apply, a Stopped robot takes Resume/ResumePending.\"\n\nIf not above, and ModeStopped: Resume/ResumePending.\n- \"Every input left over takes Proceed/Traversing.\"\n\nSo our order matches.\n\nNow, need consider whether rule5 Release/Traversing should be before rule6 (retain Brake) or after? Policy says \"For the remaining inputs, first retain Brake...\" Wait the paragraph: \"For inputs not handled so far, a robot that owns the reservation and has its entire body clear of the zone must report Release/Traversing. Clear body and released reservation are separate facts.\n\nFor the remaining inputs, first retain Brake/BrakeRequested if the current mode is BrakeRequested and stop confirmation is still absent. ...\"\n\nThus Release is before \"remaining inputs\" Brake retain. So our rule5 before rule6. Correct.\n\nWhat about if C && AtGoal && Released && OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ: rule3/4 goal release before release. Correct.\n\nWhat about if C && !AtGoal && Released && OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ: rule5 Release/Traversing. Correct, even though Released true. \"Clear body and released reservation separate.\" Yes.\n\nWhat about if C && OwnReservation && BodyClearOfZ && ModeBrakeRequested && !Halted: rule5 Release/Traversing takes precedence over Brake retain. Policy says yes because Release before remaining. Good.\n\nWhat about if C && !OwnReservation && !Released && ModeBrakeRequested && !Halted: rule6 Brake/BrakeRequested before Request/Waiting. Policy says first retain Brake if current mode BrakeRequested. So yes.\n\nWhat about if C && !OwnReservation && !Released && ModeStopped && Halted? Rule7 Request/Waiting before rule8 Resume. Policy says Request before Stopped resume. Yes.\n\nWhat about if C && OwnReservation true and Released true and BodyClear false and AtGoal false and ModeStopped: Not rule5, not rule6 unless ModeBrakeRequested, rule7 false because OwnReservation/Released not both absent, rule8 Resume/ResumePending. Is that policy? Remaining after Release: retain Brake? no. Request? no. Stopped robot takes Resume. Yes. Even though OwnReservation true/Released true, mode stopped -> Resume. Good.\n\nWhat about if C && OwnReservation true and Released true and BodyClear false and ModeIdle: leftover Proceed/Traversing. Yes.\n\nWhat about if C && !OwnReservation && Released true and ModeStopped: rule7 false (Released true), rule8 Resume. Good.\n\nWhat about if C && !OwnReservation && Released false and ModeStopped: rule7 Request/Waiting before Resume. Good.\n\nWhat about if !C && ModeStopped && Halted false: rule2 Brake/BrakeRequested, not Resume. Correct.\n\nWhat about if !C && Halted true && ModeBrakeRequested: rule1 Brake/Stopped. Correct.\n\nNow, do we need to use \"ModeDone\" anywhere? No, leftover Proceed/Traversing even in Done. Policy says no terminal shortcut. If ModeDone and C and not special, Proceed/Traversing. Our rule9. If ModeDone and !C, failure Brake. Good.\n\nNow, possible hidden issue: \"At most 32 total branches are allowed; the per-function limit of twelve still applies.\" Branches might mean each rule with condition? We have 12. Good.\n\nNeed ensure JSON has no duplicate keys. We'll write.\n\nNeed maybe include \"when\": \"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked and Halted\"? Precedence: and before or, so this parses as (not ObservationUsable) or (not TaskActive) or (PedestrianBlocked and Halted). That's wrong. Need parentheses. We'll use parentheses explicitly:\n\"when\": \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted\"\n\"when\": \"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and not Halted\"\nRule3: \"AtGoal and Released and Halted\" (and is left-assoc; okay). But if Halted true. Rule4: \"AtGoal and Released and not Halted\". Good.\nRule5: \"OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\"\nRule6: \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\"\nRule7: \"not OwnReservation and not Released\" (means both absent). Need parentheses? and precedence; not binds tighter. Good.\nRule8: \"ModeStopped\"\nRule9: \"True\"\n\nNeed ensure step expressions can refer to TaskActive, ObservationUsable, PedestrianBlocked, OwnReservation, Released, BodyClearOfZ, Halted, AtGoal, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped. We don't use ModeIdle etc. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The prompt says \"Expressions allow only True, False, the permitted Boolean names, not, and, or, and parentheses, with Python Boolean precedence.\" It might not allow string \"True\" as name? Yes. We use exactly \"True\" in rule9. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"No calls, comparisons, arithmetic, attributes, new names, or assignment.\" Our expressions okay.\n\nPotential issue: \"A condition has at most 512 characters, 64 Python syntax-tree nodes and expression depth eight.\" Our longest maybe rule1 ~80 chars, nodes small. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"select cannot use any step or mode names; step cannot use any select names.\" Our sel